        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "6c9d6950-c654-56c3-8b40-8476bb61a541",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Magnus Barefoot, Magnus Olafsson\n- Places: Norway, British Isles, Ireland, Kingdom of the Isles, Dublin, Orkney, Shetland\n- Roles: King of Norway, overlords\n- Concepts: Suðreyjar, Southern Isles, Norðreyjar, Northern Isles\n- Infrastructure: King Magnus' Halt railway station\n\nFacts:\n- Magnus Olafsson, better known as Magnus Barefoot, was born in 1073 and died on 24 August 1103.\n- Magnus Barefoot ruled as King of Norway, styled Magnus III, from 1093 until 1103.\n- Magnus Barefoot's reign was marked by aggressive military campaigns and conquest in Norse-dominated parts of the British Isles and Ireland.\n- Magnus Barefoot extended his rule to the Kingdom of the Isles and Dublin.\n- The islands of the Kingdom of the Isles were known to the Norse as the Suðreyjar, meaning the Southern Isles, in contrast to the Norðreyjar, or Northern Isles, of Orkney and Shetland.\n- The rulers of the Kingdom of the Isles were sometimes independent of external control, but for much of the period they had overlords in Norway, Ireland, England, Scotland, or Orkney.\n- King Magnus' Halt railway station takes its name from the nearby grave of Viking king Magnus Barefoot.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "011761f0-58f5-54a1-9b77-14b67db3c6f2",
        "text": "Page: Magnus Barefoot. Sentence: Magnus Olafsson (Old Norse: \"Magnús Óláfsson\", Norwegian: \"Magnus Olavsson\"; 1073 – 24 August 1103), better known as Magnus Barefoot (Old Norse: \"Magnús berfœttr\", Norwegian: \"Magnus Berrføtt\"), was King of Norway (as Magnus III) from 1093 until his death in 1103.\nPage: Magnus Barefoot. Sentence: His reign was marked by aggressive military campaigns and conquest, particularly in the Norse-dominated parts of the British Isles and Ireland, where he extended his rule to the Kingdom of the Isles and Dublin.\nPage: Kingdom of the Isles. Sentence: The islands were known to the Norse as the \"Suðreyjar\", or \"Southern Isles\" as distinct from the \"Norðreyjar\" or Northern Isles of Orkney and Shetland.\nPage: Kingdom of the Isles. Sentence: At times the rulers were independent of external control, although for much of the period they had overlords in Norway, Ireland, England, Scotland or Orkney.\nPage: King Magnus' Halt railway station. Sentence: It takes its name from the nearby grave of Viking King Magnus Barefoot.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
